155 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 387 0 R/E(Vowel pronunciation is one of the most persistent challenges in English language teaching, yet many teachers feel under-prepared to diagnose or train vowel issues with confidence. This workshop introduces a practical, teacher-friendly approach to phonetics and phonology using the IPA chart, vowel space awareness, and simple self-recording on mobile phones.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

156 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 387 0 R/E(Designed for beginner and experienced ESL teachers alike, the session focuses on seeing and hearing vowel differences clearly\204across different learner backgrounds\204without requiring technical knowledge or specialist software. Participants will explore how English vowel spaces differ across countries, how first-language vowel systems influence English pronunciation, and how teachers can train vowels more effectively in class.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

157 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 387 0 R/E(The workshop is hands-on and reflective. Teachers will record short speech samples on their phones, use the IPA chart to analyse their own vowel production, and practice simple classroom techniques for helping students notice and adjust vowel quality. The aim is to build confidence, accuracy, and awareness\204so teachers can move beyond \215repeat after me\216 and towards informed, effective pronunciation teaching.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

158 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 388 0 R/E(Exam preparation courses can be demanding for both students and teachers. Time pressure, high expectations, and repeated exposure to familiar task types can make sustaining learner engagement over time challenging. Finding ways to balance effective exam training with enjoyable learning experiences is therefore an important consideration for these courses.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

159 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 388 0 R/E(This workshop presents a range of engaging approaches to practising tasks from the Reading and Use of English, Listening, and Speaking papers. Participants will explore interactive activities that encourage collaboration, discussion, and active language use, while still preparing learners effectively for the exam. While rooted in exam preparation, the activities shown can be readily adapted for other language exam formats and for General English teaching. These activities promote strategic thinking, deeper language awareness, and learner confidence, supporting success in exams as well as in real-world communication. The session demonstrates that effective exam preparation does not have to come at the expense of enjoyment in the classroom.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

160 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 389 0 R/E(Writing is a highly time intensive skill to work on in class, and to correct and provide meaningful feedback on. This can lead to shallow learning, underdeveloped skills, and missed opportunities to improve students' overall ability. This presentation covers the rationale for valuing writing, shares some engaging and effective writing activities, and suggests how to make things sustainable for we overworked teachers - all in ways that don't rely on AI tools. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

161 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 390 0 R/T(Nathaniel Mitchell is formally trained in Phonetics at Griffith University, lecturing into phonetics and phonology from 2013 until 2018. His Honors project specifically looked at pronunciation of Australian English and worked on the Western Australian English Pronunciation project with Griffith University. )/E(Nathaniel Mitchell is formally trained in Phonetics at Griffith University, lecturing into phonetics and phonology from 2013 until 2018. His Honors project specifically looked at pronunciation of Australian English and worked on the Western Australian English Pronunciation project with Griffith University. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

162 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 391 0 R/T(Bec Hungerford has over 15 years\220 experience teaching English both in Australia and overseas, much of it focused on Cambridge exam preparation. She is passionate about fostering learner autonomy and encouraging curiosity about language and the wider world. She strongly believes that effective teaching should be engaging and human \204 and that motivating students and colleagues with chocolate and home-baked goods is a perfectly valid pedagogical strategy.)/E(Bec Hungerford has over 15 years\220 experience teaching English both in Australia and overseas, much of it focused on Cambridge exam preparation. She is passionate about fostering learner autonomy and encouraging curiosity about language and the wider world. She strongly believes that effective teaching should be engaging and human \204 and that motivating students and colleagues with chocolate and home-baked goods is a perfectly valid pedagogical strategy.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

163 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 392 0 R/T(Neridah got her CELTA some 14 years ago with the intention of working overseas long enough to sound interesting at parties. She has gone on to love of the challenge of EAP and IELTs courses and completed a Masters of Teaching in 2021. Her great loves are books, trivia, and teachable moments. )/E(Neridah got her CELTA some 14 years ago with the intention of working overseas long enough to sound interesting at parties. She has gone on to love of the challenge of EAP and IELTs courses and completed a Masters of Teaching in 2021. Her great loves are books, trivia, and teachable moments. )/Pg 14 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

