168 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/P 393 0 R/T(Good student or good learner? Building learner agency in short ELICOS courses - Presented by Chiaki Lawler \(11.30-12.30\))/E(Good student or good learner? Building learner agency in short ELICOS courses - Presented by Chiaki Lawler \(11.30-12.30\))/Pg 15 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

169 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/P 394 0 R/T(Tech as a thinking tool: Supporting imagination, criticality, and care in ELT - Presented byJohn Day & Katie Clancy \(11.30-12.30\))/E(Tech as a thinking tool: Supporting imagination, criticality, and care in ELT - Presented byJohn Day & Katie Clancy \(11.30-12.30\))/Pg 15 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

170 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/P 395 0 R/T(Grounding AI-mediated writing feedback in theory: The Three Ts model for informed educational practice - Presented by Devrim Yilmaz \(11.30-12.00\))/E(Grounding AI-mediated writing feedback in theory: The Three Ts model for informed educational practice - Presented by Devrim Yilmaz \(11.30-12.00\))/Pg 15 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

171 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 396 0 R/E(It is not uncommon to see students begin their English language learning journey at an Australian ELICOS with a preconceived belief about what it means to be a good \217student.\220 Being quiet, obedient, accuracy-focused, and dependent on the teacher may be the norm in some cultures. While understandable, these behaviours can limit participation, risk-taking, and meaningful communication in Australian classrooms, making learner agency essential.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

172 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 396 0 R/E(With recent student visa regulations and decreased international enrolments, some providers have noticed increased interest in short, under 12 week programs on visitor visas. Although there is no conclusive national evidence that short-term cohorts are rising across the ELICOS sector, Department of Home Affairs data shows more visitor visa entrants and fewer student visa holders. This may contribute to local increases in short-term cohorts, but it remains anecdotal rather than a confirmed national trend. Still, concentrated engagement is vital to build confidence and support proactive learning within compressed timeframes.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

173 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 396 0 R/E(In this interactive workshop, participants will explore practical, research-informed strategies to guide learners from passive to proactive. Through task redesign, teacher talk reframing, micro routines, and culturally informed discussion, participants will gain techniques to enhance autonomy, participation, and willingness to communicate\204regardless of background or visa type.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

174 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 397 0 R/E<>/Pg 15 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

175 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 397 0 R/E(The session is structured around three interrelated principles: imagination \(supporting learners to generate and shape meaning\), criticality \(supporting evaluation and judgement\), and care \(supporting confidence, agency, and inclusion\). The workshop emphasises that affective support and ethical decision-making cannot be automated and remain the responsibility of teachers.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

176 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 397 0 R/E(Participants engage in small-group, task-based activities to analyse classroom AI scenarios and redesign them using CAC constraints: learners think first, use AI to enhance ideas, and return to human judgement to evaluate and refine outcomes. Rather than focusing on specific tools, the workshop highlights how task design and sequencing determine whether AI extends or replaces thinking. Participants leave with a practical framework and design principles for responsible AI use. This workshop is not about using more technology; it is about using it carefully.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

177 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 398 0 R/E(Generative Artificial Intelligence \(GenAI\) is increasingly used to provide feedback on student writing due to its efficiency and capacity for wider implementation. However, despite its technological capabilities, AI-generated feedback risks becoming unfocused or pedagogically superficial if it is not grounded in educational research, linguistic conceptualisation, and learning theory. This presentation argues that effective AI-mediated feedback must be driven by pedagogy rather than technology. Building on earlier work that categorised written feedback according to degrees of explicitness and the presence of rationale, the presentation introduces a recently developed framework: the Three Ts of Feedback. This model conceptualises feedback across three interconnected dimensions: type, target and text level, as these dimensions provide practitioners with a structured lens for analysing feedback practices and identifying patterns shaped by learner proficiency, age, and educational context. The presentation demonstrates how GenAI can be purposefully used to create a pedagogically informed comment bank aligned with the Three Ts framework. In essence, GenAI is positioned not as a replacement for teacher judgement but as an educational assistant that supports consistent, focused, and theory-driven feedback practices while maintaining learner-centred principles.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

178 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 399 0 R/T(Chiaki has over 25 years of experience catering to students from diverse backgrounds and ages. She has taught at various private and government institutions in Australia and Japan. She specialises in pre and in-service teacher training. Her focus as an educator is World Englishes, cross-cultural identity, and communication. At SCU College, she is a teaching scholar, and her primary role is to oversee the English Language Program and provide student support across campuses.)/E(Chiaki has over 25 years of experience catering to students from diverse backgrounds and ages. She has taught at various private and government institutions in Australia and Japan. She specialises in pre and in-service teacher training. Her focus as an educator is World Englishes, cross-cultural identity, and communication. At SCU College, she is a teaching scholar, and her primary role is to oversee the English Language Program and provide student support across campuses.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

179 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 400 0 R/T(John Day is an English language teacher at UQ College with over ten years\220 experience in TESOL teaching, teacher training, and academic leadership in Australia and China. Alongside his ongoing classroom teaching, he contributes to curriculum design, quality assurance, and professional development, and has delivered workshops on pedagogy and educational technology. He is particularly interested in exploring how AI can be integrated into TESOL in ways that strengthen learner thinking, confidence, and meaningful communication.)/E(John Day is an English language teacher at UQ College with over ten years\220 experience in TESOL teaching, teacher training, and academic leadership in Australia and China. Alongside his ongoing classroom teaching, he contributes to curriculum design, quality assurance, and professional development, and has delivered workshops on pedagogy and educational technology. He is particularly interested in exploring how AI can be integrated into TESOL in ways that strengthen learner thinking, confidence, and meaningful communication.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

180 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 400 0 R/T(Katie Clancy has worked across Australian mainstream schools and English programs in South America, and has spent the past 10 years at UQ College. Her work spans university preparation programs and Learning Advice, supporting diverse learners in academic contexts. Grounded in cross-cultural experience, Katie is committed to equity, student voice, and reflective practice. She values curiosity, collaboration, and ongoing professional growth, and is interested in how educational technology and AI can support confidence, critical thinking, and independent academic identities.)/E(Katie Clancy has worked across Australian mainstream schools and English programs in South America, and has spent the past 10 years at UQ College. Her work spans university preparation programs and Learning Advice, supporting diverse learners in academic contexts. Grounded in cross-cultural experience, Katie is committed to equity, student voice, and reflective practice. She values curiosity, collaboration, and ongoing professional growth, and is interested in how educational technology and AI can support confidence, critical thinking, and independent academic identities.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

181 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 401 0 R/T(An experienced educator and teacher educator specialising in EAP, TESOL, and Applied Linguistics, with a strong focus on writing pedagogy and feedback. He has extensive experience in curriculum design, pedagogical innovation, and academic language development in tertiary contexts. His work emphasises culturally responsive teaching, plurilingualism, and theory-informed use of Generative AI in language education. He has led teams, developed institutional resources, and taught across face-to-face, blended, and online programs. His current interests include feedback literacy, teacher development, and technology-enhanced learning.)/E(An experienced educator and teacher educator specialising in EAP, TESOL, and Applied Linguistics, with a strong focus on writing pedagogy and feedback. He has extensive experience in curriculum design, pedagogical innovation, and academic language development in tertiary contexts. His work emphasises culturally responsive teaching, plurilingualism, and theory-informed use of Generative AI in language education. He has led teams, developed institutional resources, and taught across face-to-face, blended, and online programs. His current interests include feedback literacy, teacher development, and technology-enhanced learning.)/Pg 15 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

