207 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/P 413 0 R/T(What if the teacher\220s guide was actually useful? - Presented by Niki Joseph \(1.30-2.00\))/E(What if the teacher\220s guide was actually useful? - Presented by Niki Joseph \(1.30-2.00\))/Pg 17 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

208 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H6/P 414 0 R/T(As generative AI reshapes student writing and learning, pathways programs face growing pressure to uphold academic integrity while preparing students for university expectations. This session argues that key intellectual and academic skills that are vital for students\220 ability to engage ethically with AI are often assumed rather than explicitly taught in pathways curricula. Drawing on higher education practice and current research in progress, the presentation suggests opportunities for pathway programs to take a steering role in preparing students for first-year university demands in relation to engagement with AI. It will argue that doing so requires educators to harness the critical thinking capabilities already present in the cohort, as well as provide students with the practical skills required to assess the validity of source material and demonstrate effective argumentation. Participants will explore practical strategies for embedding critical thinking through scaffolded tasks, metacognitive reflection, and assessment design that prioritises process over product. )/E(As generative AI reshapes student writing and learning, pathways programs face growing pressure to uphold academic integrity while preparing students for university expectations. This session argues that key intellectual and academic skills that are vital for students\220 ability to engage ethically with AI are often assumed rather than explicitly taught in pathways curricula. Drawing on higher education practice and current research in progress, the presentation suggests opportunities for pathway programs to take a steering role in preparing students for first-year university demands in relation to engagement with AI. It will argue that doing so requires educators to harness the critical thinking capabilities already present in the cohort, as well as provide students with the practical skills required to assess the validity of source material and demonstrate effective argumentation. Participants will explore practical strategies for embedding critical thinking through scaffolded tasks, metacognitive reflection, and assessment design that prioritises process over product. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

209 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H6/P 415 0 R/T<>/E<>/Pg 17 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

210 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H6/P 416 0 R/T(Teacher\220s Guides are written for teachers, yet they are frequently underused or overlooked. At best, they might be consulted to check answer keys, at worst, teachers may not even receive a copy.  However, most Teacher\220s Guides are packed with resources which, when used or adapted, can save valuable preparation time, enrich lessons and even serve as a form of ongoing professional development. This is a practical workshop, and participants will engage in hands-on activities including trying out the writing of their own teacher\220s notes for an activity and also discovering some hidden gems within the Guides. There will also be plenty of opportunities to share experiences and ideas.  Participants will leave with a toolkit of creative, ready-to-use ideas that will encourage closer reading of Teacher\220s Guides, enhanced coursebook use and inspiration for classroom practice.)/E(Teacher\220s Guides are written for teachers, yet they are frequently underused or overlooked. At best, they might be consulted to check answer keys, at worst, teachers may not even receive a copy.  However, most Teacher\220s Guides are packed with resources which, when used or adapted, can save valuable preparation time, enrich lessons and even serve as a form of ongoing professional development. This is a practical workshop, and participants will engage in hands-on activities including trying out the writing of their own teacher\220s notes for an activity and also discovering some hidden gems within the Guides. There will also be plenty of opportunities to share experiences and ideas.  Participants will leave with a toolkit of creative, ready-to-use ideas that will encourage closer reading of Teacher\220s Guides, enhanced coursebook use and inspiration for classroom practice.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

211 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 417 0 R/E(Dr Mia Tarau is a creative and experienced educator, active across pathways and tertiary education contexts in Australia and New Zealand. Her expertise spans policy research, teaching and learning, and inclusive curriculum design, fostering the development of academic integrity and academic skills across Humanities, language education, and applied linguistics. Her current collaborative project focuses on supporting students in using AI for learning in critical, ethical, and responsible ways, while strengthening their confidence and ownership of academic voice.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

212 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 417 0 R/E(Lorna Clark is an experienced educator and program coordinator who has worked across pathways and tertiary education contexts in Australia. Her PhD research focuses on exploring the epistemology of international students as it relates to critical and connected ways of knowing. Her areas of interest include critical thinking, pedagogy and inclusive curriculum design. This collaboration focuses on supporting students in using AI for learning in critical, ethical, and responsible ways, while strengthening their confidence and ownership of academic voice.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

213 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 417 0 R/E(Chanelle Swanepoel is a Business and Economics educator whose philosophy centres on relationships, empathy and high expectations, helping learners belong and build confidence. With experience across South Africa and Australia in high school and tertiary enabling pathway programs, she designs inclusive, future-focused learning that strengthens language and digital literacy. Her practice integrates design thinking, multimodal assessment and purposeful technology. She collaborates on projects supporting students to use AI critically, ethically and responsibly, strengthening academic voice and ownership.)/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

