<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 423 0 R/E<>/Pg 18 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

227 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 423 0 R/E<>/Pg 18 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

228 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 423 0 R/E(Teachers will leave with prompt templates, replicable workflows, and the confidence to start using Copilot to enhance\204not replace\204their professional expertise.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

229 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 424 0 R/E(Modal verbs are often presented as a discrete grammar point, taught in a single unit and assessed shortly afterwards. However, for many learners, modals are not simply a matter of form and function. They require the brain to process degrees of certainty, politeness, social distance, and implied meaning. These are concepts that may not be encoded in the same way in a learner\220s first language, or may be used differently even in languages that have modal systems.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

230 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 424 0 R/E(This session explores what happens cognitively and linguistically when learners encounter English modals, particularly those whose first languages express obligation, possibility, or politeness through different grammatical or pragmatic means. Drawing on classroom examples, the presentation will examine how meaning is negotiated, why learners often interpret modals literally, and how cultural expectations about directness and politeness influence both production and interpretation.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

231 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 424 0 R/E(Participants will also reflect on teaching practices: How effective is teaching modals as a single unit within a longer course? What kinds of explanations and activities help learners grasp not only meaning, but nuance?)/Pg 18 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

232 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 424 0 R/E(The session will provide practical strategies to support deeper understanding of modals in multilingual classrooms and to improve clarity, confidence, and intercultural communication.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

233 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 425 0 R/E(PSA:  Only for 'newbies' aka. new teachers to ELICOS, and returning ones, ok!  )/Pg 18 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

234 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 425 0 R/E(Navigating your way as a 'newbie' into sector can be exciting and daunting at the same time. We are both passionate and experienced professionals, ready to mentor and share what we have learnt along the way. We'll have an open discussion to help you build your networks, value PD.  Networking.  Understanding your place in the sector, for now and beyond. Ask questions and share in our safe space!)/Pg 18 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

235 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 426 0 R/T<>/E<>/Pg 18 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

236 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 427 0 R/T(Sermin Erden is an experienced language educator with a strong interest in the relationship between language, cognition, and communication in multilingual contexts. Her previous work explored how linguistic differences are often mistaken for cultural differences in second language learning and teaching. Building on this, her current focus brings language and culture together through the lens of grammar, examining how meaning, nuance, and social expectations are encoded and taught in English. She is passionate about helping teachers better understand multilingual learners and classroom communication.)/E(Sermin Erden is an experienced language educator with a strong interest in the relationship between language, cognition, and communication in multilingual contexts. Her previous work explored how linguistic differences are often mistaken for cultural differences in second language learning and teaching. Building on this, her current focus brings language and culture together through the lens of grammar, examining how meaning, nuance, and social expectations are encoded and taught in English. She is passionate about helping teachers better understand multilingual learners and classroom communication.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

237 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 428 0 R/T(Jo Kwai is a QCT registered, experienced teacher and has worked in the schools sector \(public and private\), ELICOS, TAFE AMEP, VET Trainer and Assessor \(TESOL\); Jo has also been an Academic Manager, and is currently on the secondary school team at St Peters Lutheran College, as a CLA-EALD, and International Student Coordinator.  She is also a current board member of the federal govt Tuition Protection Service \(TPS\). )/E(Jo Kwai is a QCT registered, experienced teacher and has worked in the schools sector \(public and private\), ELICOS, TAFE AMEP, VET Trainer and Assessor \(TESOL\); Jo has also been an Academic Manager, and is currently on the secondary school team at St Peters Lutheran College, as a CLA-EALD, and International Student Coordinator.  She is also a current board member of the federal govt Tuition Protection Service \(TPS\). )/Pg 18 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

238 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 428 0 R/T(Donna Cook is the In-Country manager, Cambridge. She is an experienced senior manager with over 20 years of experience in the higher education sector. She also served on the English Australia Board for over 10 years, and has an IEAA Fellowship.  Donna previously was the Senior Academic Manager, Australian Catholic University.)/E(Donna Cook is the In-Country manager, Cambridge. She is an experienced senior manager with over 20 years of experience in the higher education sector. She also served on the English Australia Board for over 10 years, and has an IEAA Fellowship.  Donna previously was the Senior Academic Manager, Australian Catholic University.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

